     1	# Versioning Policy
     2	
     3	<!-- last-reviewed: 2026-09-08 v1.4.0 -->
     4	
     5	> **TL;DR** — SemVer at the **Compliance SPEC level** (`SPEC/v1/`),
     6	> not at every internal symbol. Tagged releases mark SPEC-level
     7	> stability; untagged commits on `main` may include experimental
     8	> additions behind kill switches.
     9	
    10	## Scope of "the version"
    11	
    12	`VERSION` at the repo root is the **single source of truth** for the
    13	SPEC level the working tree complies with. It moves on:
    14	
    15	- Any change to a file under `SPEC/v1/` (additive or breaking)
    16	- Any change that introduces a **new trust boundary** an adopter must
    17	  accept (e.g. ADR-051 added the SHA-pinned skill-by-reference path → MINOR bump)
    18	- Any change to canonical-edit-guarded paths that alters governance
    19	  semantics (mechanically gated by `check_canonical_edit.py`)
    20	
    21	It does **not** move on:
    22	
    23	- Doc improvements that don't change a contract
    24	- New skills added under `.claude/skills/core/` or
    25	  `.claude/skills/domains/<domain>/skills/` (skills are content, not contract)
    26	- New plans, ADRs in PROPOSED state, or research notes
    27	- Test additions, refactors that preserve byte-identity of fixtures
    28	- New optional CLI tools (`ceo-cost`, `ceo-health`, etc.) — additive,
    29	  no SPEC change
    30	
    31	## SemVer semantics at SPEC level
    32	
    33	Format: `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-rc.N]`.
    34	
    35	### MAJOR — breaking SPEC change
    36	
    37	Bumped when a published `SPEC/v1/*.schema.md` removes or renames a
    38	field, drops a required event type, or changes type semantics in a
    39	way that breaks consumers.
    40	
    41	**Historic example:** none yet within v1. The `v1` SPEC is intended
    42	to remain stable through `v2.x`. A `v2/` SPEC would be created
    43	alongside `v1/` for the transition window per ADR-005 §Migration.
    44	
    45	### MINOR — additive SPEC change OR new trust boundary
    46	
    47	Bumped when:
    48	
    49	- A new field is added to an existing event (always additive — see
    50	  `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md` §Additivity)
    51	- A new event type is added (e.g. `injection_flag` in v2.1)
    52	- A new trust boundary is introduced that adopters must understand
    53	  (e.g. ADR-051 skill-by-reference → `v1.5 → v1.6`)
    54	- A new SPEC file is published under `SPEC/v1/`
    55	
    56	**Historic examples (public series — `v1.0.0` is the public genesis;
    57	pre-genesis history is condensed into CHANGELOG `[1.0.0]`):**
    58	
    59	| Bump | What changed |
    60	|------|--------------|
    61	| `v1.0.0 → v1.1.0` | PLAN-153/155/156 — two new host harnesses (Codex CLI, Grok Build) = new trust boundaries an adopter must accept; cross-vendor audit council; gated learning loop. |
    62	| `v1.1.0 → v1.2.0` | PLAN-160/161/163/164 — Claude 5 model registry (ADR-181, additive `model:` contract), Codex payload-pin enforcement (ADR-182), pair-rail timeout contract (ADR-110-AMEND-1), new typed audit actions. |
    63	| `v1.2.0 → v1.3.0` | PLAN-162/165 — night-mode posture toggle (new trust boundary, ADR-185); sentinel-unlock provenance inside git worktrees (ADR-119 Invariant 5 — closes in-window self-authored-sentinel escalation); fail-CLOSED matcher deadline (ADR-186); pair-rail 180/210 + `timeout_ms` (ADR-110-AMEND-2). |
    64	
    65	### PATCH — bug fixes, additive features within SPEC
    66	
    67	Bumped for:
    68	
    69	- Hook bug fixes (e.g. the Session 32 redaction-check fix)
    70	- Additive optional fields without schema changes
    71	- Performance improvements without behavioral changes
    72	- Documentation that aligns with existing contract
    73	- Test infrastructure expansion
    74	
    75	**Historic example:** `v1.0.0 → v1.0.1` (2026-07-06) — the PLAN-152
    76	hardening sweep: hook fixes and gate hardening with no SPEC or
    77	trust-boundary change. PATCH is available when needed, not mandatory.
    78	
    79	### Pre-release (`-rc.N`) — release candidate
    80	
    81	Every MAJOR or MINOR bump goes through a **mandatory RC-to-GA hold**
    82	mechanically enforced by `.github/workflows/release.yml` (the
    83	"Assert 24h Codex re-pass window" step). Per ADR-103 the window is
    84	**24 hours**, not a calendar settle period: it bounds the maximum
    85	turnaround for the external Codex re-pass (anti same-LLM-bias per
    86	ADR-095 §gate-#6). The flow:
    87	
    88	1. Cut `vMAJOR.MINOR.0-rc.1` tag on `main` HEAD.
    89	2. RC hold begins; the Codex re-pass runs against the RC.
    90	3. If a fix lands during the hold, cut `-rc.2`. The 24h clock
    91	   restarts from the latest RC.
    92	4. At least 24h after the latest RC, with green CI, the Owner cuts
    93	   the GA tag (`vMAJOR.MINOR.0`). `release.yml` rejects a GA tag cut
    94	   < 24h after its RC (creator-date delta < 86400 s), or with no
    95	   prior RC tag at all.
    96	
    97	**The 24h hold is mechanical, not a flag.** There is no
    98	`--fail-if-delta-lt-7d`; the gate computes the creator-date delta
    99	between the GA tag and its most-recent `-rc.*` tag. During the
   100	pre-GA phase (`adopter_count=0`) the hold can be waived via an
   101	Owner-signed entry in `.claude/governance/governance-waivers.yaml`.
   102	
   103	PATCH releases use the same `-rc` flow and the same mechanical 24h
   104	floor; a security fix per [`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md) may ship as
   105	soon as the 24h re-pass window clears.
   106	
   107	## What "tag" means
   108	
   109	Three artifacts move together at a tag:
   110	
   111	1. `VERSION` at repo root reflects the new version number.
   112	2. `npm/package.json` `version` matches (npm publishes on tag via
   113	   `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml`).
   114	3. `CHANGELOG.md` entry exists under `## [N.N.N] - YYYY-MM-DD` (the
   115	   heading carries the bare version, with no `v` prefix, and for an RC
   116	   it is the BASE version — `release.sh preflight` greps
   117	   `^## \[<base>\]`).
   118	
   119	The tag is the single event. If any of these three is out of sync at
   120	tag time, `release.yml` fails.
   121	
   122	## Adopter pinning
   123	
   124	Adopters pin a specific framework version via:
   125	
   126	```bash
   127	# From the adopter project root (replace vX.Y.Z with the desired tag)
   128	bash scripts/upgrade.sh --pin vX.Y.Z
   129	```
   130	
   131	Behavior of `--pin`:
   132	
   133	- Refuses to upgrade if the adopter has uncommitted changes under
   134	  `.claude/`. Resolve via commit, stash, or `git checkout`.
   135	- Has no MAJOR-boundary guard and no `--allow-major` flag — it
   136	  checks out exactly the tag you pass (pre-2.0, there is no MAJOR
   137	  boundary to cross yet).
   138	- Preserves adopter overrides per `upgrade.sh upgrade_agents_canonical_only`
   139	  (per ADR-052 §Adopter override).
   140	- Backs up the previous install to `.claude.bak/<timestamp>/` for
   141	  manual recovery.
   142	
   143	A pin is **one-shot, not durable**: `--pin` checks out exactly that tag
   144	for that upgrade run, and nothing replays it afterwards — a later
   145	`bash scripts/upgrade.sh` without `--pin` follows the current default
   146	source, not the previously pinned tag. If you want to stay pinned,
   147	pass `--pin vX.Y.Z` explicitly on every upgrade (your npm
   148	`package.json` / lockfile records the version you installed and is a
   149	good place to look it up).
   150	
   151	See [`docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md`](docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md) for the
   152	full step-by-step adopter playbook.
   153	
   154	## Model ID bumps (Anthropic model family changes)
   155	
   156	The framework names specific Claude model IDs in canonical-5 native
   157	agent frontmatter (per ADR-052; current IDs set by the ADR-181
   158	Claude 5 refresh, PLAN-163):
   159	
   160	- `claude-fable-5` (code-reviewer + security-engineer)
   161	- `claude-sonnet-4-6` (qa-architect + performance-engineer + devops)
   162	
   163	When Anthropic releases the next model family, the IDs become stale.
   164	The bump process is **not silent** — it was exercised in full for the
   165	Claude 5 family (PLAN-163 / ADR-181, shipped in v1.2.0):
   166	
   167	1. Benchmark the new model against canonical-5 rubrics
   168	   (`.claude/plans/PLAN-020/rubrics/<archetype>.yaml`). Pass-rate
   169	   must be ≥ current baseline.
   170	2. Run `benchmarks/replay.py` on
   171	   `replay-fixtures/plan-019-wave-2a.jsonl`. Spawn-prompt token
   172	   delta must not regress.
   173	3. Author an `ADR-NNN` referencing ADR-052 + benchmark evidence.
   174	4. Update frontmatter `model:` fields in `.claude/agents/<slug>.md`.
   175	5. Bump the audit-log schema (e.g. `v2.9`) if the new model exposes
   176	   additional `usage_metadata` fields.
   177	6. The bump is a MINOR version (additive contract for adopters who
   178	   read `model:` from the audit log).
   179	
   180	Adopters who want a different model split override the `model:` field
   181	in their copy of `.claude/agents/<slug>.md`. Framework upgrades
   182	preserve overrides via the diff-detect pattern (`upgrade.sh
   183	upgrade_agents_canonical_only`).
   184	
   185	## End-of-life policy
   186	
   187	| Window | Status |
   188	|--------|--------|
   189	| Current MINOR (`v1.4.x`) | Full support — features + security + bug fixes |
   190	| Previous MINOR (`v1.3.x`) | Security-only patches for **6 months** after the next MINOR ships |
   191	| Older (`v1.0.x`, `v1.1.x`) | Best-effort — we describe the upgrade path; no back-ports |
   192	
   193	Upgrade via `bash scripts/upgrade.sh --pin vX.Y.Z` (consult
   194	CHANGELOG.md for the sequence). Skipping a MINOR is supported across
   195	the v1 series but discouraged because each MINOR adds tests and
   196	adopter-facing behavior worth dogfooding individually.
   197	
   198	## Backward compatibility within a MINOR
   199	
   200	A `vX.Y.Z` install always understands an audit-log written by an
   201	older `vX.Y.W` install. The reverse is **not** guaranteed —
   202	forward-rolling consumers (audit-query.py, replay/replay-session.py,
   203	ceo-cost.py) are designed to ignore unknown fields per
   204	`SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md` §Consumer contract, but a downgraded
   205	install may not know about new event types.
   206	
   207	When in doubt, run a fresh `validate-governance.sh` after every
   208	upgrade to catch drift.
   209	
   210	## Cadence (best-effort)
   211	
   212	| Cadence | Real-world frequency observed in the public series (from 2026-07-01) |
   213	|---------|---------------------------------------------|
   214	| MINOR releases | ~ every 2–3 weeks so far (`v1.0.0` 07-01 → `v1.1.0` 07-13 → `v1.2.0` 07-30) |
   215	| PATCH releases | as needed (rarely batched) |
   216	| RC tags | one per intended MINOR/MAJOR (re-cut on fix during hold) |
   217	| GA tags | one per intended MINOR/MAJOR (after ≥ 24h RC hold per ADR-103) |
   218	| SPEC version (`SPEC/v1/...`) | stable through v1.x; expected v2/ at the v2.0.0 horizon |
   219	
   220	This cadence is documented for transparency, not contractual. The
   221	Owner is one person. Sprints can compress or stretch.
   222	
   223	## Where the contract really lives
   224	
   225	If a doc and the code disagree, **the SPEC files under `SPEC/v1/`
   226	win**. SPEC schemas are normative. CHANGELOG, release notes, and
   227	adopter docs are descriptive — they aim to mirror the SPEC, but the
   228	SPEC is authoritative.
   229	
   230	If you find a SPEC file that contradicts behavior, that is a bug:
   231	file via [`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md) if it has security implications,
   232	or open a GitHub issue otherwise.
   233	
   234	Last reviewed: 2026-08-04 (v1.3.0 release train — EOL window shifted to
   235	v1.3.x / v1.2.x; historic MINOR table gained the 1.2.0 -> 1.3.0 row naming
   236	the night-mode trust boundary and the sentinel-unlock provenance
   237	requirement).
