    69	
    70	## Quick start
    71	
    72	**Prerequisites:** Python ≥ 3.9, Git, and Bash. On macOS the system Bash is 3.2; install a modern one with `brew install bash` before installing.
    73	
    74	```bash
    75	# 1. Clone the framework somewhere outside your project
    76	git clone https://github.com/Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration.git
    77	cd ceo-orchestration
    78	
    79	# 2. Install it INTO your target repository
    80	./scripts/install.sh /path/to/your-app
    81	
    82	# 3. From inside your project, confirm the governance layer is live
    83	cd /path/to/your-app
    84	bash .claude/scripts/validate-governance.sh   # prints an error count; 0 = healthy
    85	```
    86	
    87	> **Install paths.** The clone-and-`install.sh` flow above is the supported path today. A one-step npm shim is also available — `npx ceo-orchestration /path/to/your-app` (published to npm with SLSA **Level 2** provenance via `npm publish --provenance`; Level 3 is out of scope — see `npm/INTEGRITY.md`).
    88	
    89	By default the installer copies the core and frontend skill profiles and the governance hooks. Select profiles and stack hooks explicitly:
    90	
    91	```bash
    92	./scripts/install.sh /path/to/your-app --profile core,frontend,fintech --stack node
    93	```
    94	
    95	Two install modes:
    96	
    97	- `--ceremony maintainer` (default): full governance, including signed-edit ceremonies on protected paths.
    98	- `--ceremony user`: no signing ceremonies and writes only under `.claude/` — with the default Claude
    99	  harness (`--harness codex|grok` adds enforcing edit gates and files outside `.claude/`; that
   100	  composition is unsupported with `user` in this rc — signed condition 84). Most hooks are advisory; the
   101	  few that can block a change are named, with their kill-switch, under `blocking_inclusions` in
   102	  `templates/settings/settings.user.json`. Good for a low-friction trial.
   103	
   104	To verify the safety guards actually block what they claim, run the in-process self-test (hermetic, no network, no cost):
   105	
   106	```bash
   107	# from inside your installed project, in Claude Code
   108	/self-test
   109	```
   110	
